# Independently authored PG ALTER SYSTEM + SHOW
# wire-divergence sentinel slice for the GPL
# MTR-format runner. NOT copied or adapted from
# MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (do NOT duplicate):
#   - ALTER SYSTEM SET / RESET / RESET ALL: ZERO
#     prior (grep'd ALL .test files).
#   - pg_reload_conf: ZERO prior.
#   - SHOW work_mem (GUC name): ZERO prior.
#   - SHOW ALL: ZERO prior.
#
# Pre-existing rejection shape patterns:
#   - "Expected: one of VIEW or TYPE or TABLE or INDEX
#     or ROLE or POLICY or CONNECTOR or ICEBERG or
#     SCHEMA or USER or OPERATOR, found: <KW>" — pre-
#     existing fingerprint for the ALTER-after parser
#     dispatch (pg_misc_ddl_sentinel:10-14 DEFAULT;
#     event_use_partition_sentinel:6 EVENT; pg_pubsub
#     PUBLICATION; sequence_basic_extra:50 comment).
#     SYSTEM is a new keyword witness.
#   - "execution error: unknown function: <FN>" —
#     pre-existing fingerprint. pg_reload_conf is a
#     new function-name witness.
#   - "unsupported statement: sqlparser::ast::Statement"
#     — Shape (c) catch-all from PR #371. SHOW ALL on
#     pgwire lands here (parses as Statement::ShowAll,
#     unmatched).
#
# This slice pins:
#
#   ALTER SYSTEM SET / RESET / RESET ALL — pre-
#   existing "Expected: one of VIEW or TYPE..."
#   enumeration shape. New SYSTEM keyword witness.
#   All three forms reject identically because the
#   SYSTEM token is rejected at the ALTER-dispatch
#   point BEFORE the SET/RESET/RESET ALL is parsed.
#
#   pg_reload_conf() — unknown function (1105).
#
#   SHOW work_mem (a PG GUC name) — WIRE DIVERGENT:
#     - mysqlwire: silent empty result. The shim
#       prefix-intercept at zeta-mysqlwire/src/shim.rs:
#       1904 matches `q_lower.starts_with("show ")`,
#       runs through specific patterns (VARIABLES,
#       STATUS, character set, collation, warnings,
#       CREATE TABLE, INDEX, COLUMNS, TABLES,
#       DATABASES), and falls through to the default
#       `return Some(MySqlResult::EmptyShow)` (the
#       last statement in the SHOW block). So any
#       unrecognized SHOW returns 0 rows with no
#       error.
#     - pgwire: returns the actual GUC value (probed:
#       "64MB" after `ALTER SYSTEM SET work_mem`
#       wouldn't work, but the default value or any
#       prior SET value comes back). The pgwire SHOW
#       handler reaches zeta's GUC backing store.
#
#   SHOW ALL — WIRE DIVERGENT in the OPPOSITE
#   DIRECTION from SHOW <one-GUC>:
#     - mysqlwire: silent empty result via the same
#       EmptyShow catch-all. ALL is not a recognized
#       sub-pattern, so the default fires.
#     - pgwire: Shape (c) catch-all. sqlparser parses
#       SHOW ALL into a Statement variant that
#       pgwire's executor doesn't dispatch, falling
#       through to zeta-server/src/lib.rs's
#       statement-level final-arm Debug-repr emit.
#
#   The pinned shape in the .result file below is
#   mysqlwire's (EmptyShow / no rows). The pgwire
#   behavior is documented in this header per
#   [[feedback_wire_path_divergence_check]].
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   PG ALTER SYSTEM (the standard PG mechanism for
#   writing GUC changes to postgresql.auto.conf) is
#   entirely unreachable on zeta. Per CLAUDE.md, all
#   zeta config is programmatic via ServerConfig (no
#   TOML/YAML files), so ALTER SYSTEM has no
#   meaningful semantic on the engine. The hard
#   reject at parser level is the correct behavior.
#
#   The interesting finding is on the SHOW side:
#   mysqlwire's prefix-intercept catch-all silently
#   absorbs any SHOW <unknown-thing> as EmptyShow,
#   while pgwire either returns real GUC state (SHOW
#   <known-guc>) or errors at Shape (c) (SHOW ALL).
#   This means apps porting PG GUC introspection code
#   to the MySQL wire WILL silently get empty results
#   instead of the expected values.
#
#   Joins the running list of wire-divergence
#   patterns (PR #366, #385, #392, #396, #398, #399,
#   #405). The SHOW pattern is structurally the SAME
#   as the SET prefix-intercept pattern documented in
#   PR #399: shim.rs uses `q_lower.starts_with` /
#   `strip_prefix` for a family of statements
#   (`show `, `set `) with a catch-all return value
#   that swallows unmatched cases silently.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — ALTER SYSTEM SET /
#     RESET / RESET ALL parse-reject at the ALTER
#     dispatch enumeration. zeta config is
#     programmatic only (per CLAUDE.md), no
#     ALTER-SYSTEM-equivalent exists.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — pg_reload_conf() is
#     unknown function (1105). No equivalent exists;
#     config changes require restart.
#   # zeta-adapt: WIRE DIVERGENCE — SHOW <GUC> on
#     mysqlwire silently returns 0 rows (shim.rs:1904
#     SHOW-prefix catch-all → EmptyShow). On pgwire
#     it returns the actual GUC value. Apps probing
#     GUC state on MySQL wire MUST use pgwire instead.
#   # zeta-adapt: WIRE DIVERGENCE — SHOW ALL on
#     mysqlwire returns 0 rows (same catch-all). On
#     pgwire it errors Shape (c) ("unsupported
#     statement: sqlparser::ast::Statement").

--echo === ALTER SYSTEM SET / RESET / RESET ALL — "Expected: one of VIEW or TYPE..." SYSTEM witness ===

# All three forms reject at the SYSTEM token (col 7)
# because the ALTER-dispatch enumeration doesn't
# include SYSTEM. The SET/RESET clause is never
# reached.
--error 1064
ALTER SYSTEM SET work_mem = '64MB';
--error 1064
ALTER SYSTEM RESET work_mem;
--error 1064
ALTER SYSTEM RESET ALL;

--echo === pg_reload_conf() — unknown function (1105) ===

--error 1105
SELECT pg_reload_conf();

--echo === SHOW <GUC> — WIRE DIVERGENT (mysqlwire: EmptyShow catch-all → 0 rows; pgwire: actual GUC value) ===

# Pinned shape is mysqlwire's: silent empty result
# (no header row, no data rows). Cross-wire: pgwire
# returns the actual GUC value via its SHOW handler.
# Source: zeta-mysqlwire/src/shim.rs:1904 SHOW
# prefix-intercept with default `return
# Some(MySqlResult::EmptyShow)` for unrecognized
# sub-patterns.
SHOW work_mem;
SHOW statement_timeout;
SHOW search_path;

--echo === SHOW ALL — WIRE DIVERGENT (mysqlwire: same EmptyShow; pgwire: Shape (c) catch-all) ===

# Same mysqlwire mechanism (EmptyShow). On pgwire
# this hits the executor's Shape (c) catch-all
# because sqlparser parses SHOW ALL as a
# Statement::ShowAll variant that no pgwire arm
# dispatches.
SHOW ALL;
